Introduction to Stack
A stack is a linear data structure in which insertion and deletion of elements take place at only one
end, called the TOP.
A stack follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle. The element inserted last is removed first.

Real-life examples:
• A stack of plates in a cafeteria.
• Undo and redo operations in a text editor.
• The Back button in a web browser.
• Function calls and recursion in programming.
• Reversing a string.

